    "narrative": "For the July 27th slate, Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily into our speed-focused roster, led by **Corbin Carroll** (83.48) and **Jose Caballero** (76.02). **Corbin Carroll** is our premier play; despite the modest park factors against Keller, his elite sprint speed combined with a top-of-the-order assignment creates the highest ceiling on the board. Similarly, **Jose Caballero** provides exceptional value tonight. While his order position is lower, he benefits from a massive 13% dynamic platoon boost against Schultz, and his efficiency remains high because he avoids the base-clogging profiles that typically hinder utility-level production. We are also capitalizing on **James Wood** (76.27), whose matchup against Scherzer is surprisingly favorable; the xERA of 5.89 signals a prime opportunity for **James Wood** to leverage his 0.420 xwOBA and provide a significant boost to our HR and R totals.\n\nRegarding roster construction, the final bench spot was a tight contest between **Edwin Arroyo** (50.59) and **Zach McKinstry** (52.53). While **Edwin Arroyo** showed potential, the optimizer ultimately favored **Zach McKinstry** due to a +5% wind advantage and a cleaner platoon outlook. Our primary situational concern remains **Austin Riley** (54.19), who carries a marginal score coupled with a 31% rain risk; if the weather clears, his upside holds, but he remains the most volatile asset in today\u2019s set. Elsewhere, we are monitoring the bench closely, keeping **Travis Bazzana** and **Edwin Arroyo** ready in case of late lineup scratches, while **Trevor Story** remains sidelined on the IL. With **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Max Muncy**, **Dalton Rushing**, and **Henry Bolte** rotating through our broader organizational depth, we are positioned to maintain our SB-centric edge while maximizing the output of our primary starters like **Brice Turang** and **Isaac Paredes**, both of whom are projected for strong, high-efficiency outings.",
